BUG (hidden test name: `Convert format Z`): `dayjs(x).tz(zone)` (i.e. `proto.tz`) produces a WRONG utc offset / `format('Z')` / `format('ZZ')` / `utcOffset()`, while `valueOf()` is correct. Empirically measured (with the machine TZ set to America/New_York, i.e. `TZ=America/New_York npx jest ...`):
  dayjs('2014-12-01T12:00:00Z').tz('America/Los_Angeles') -> dayjs offset -420 (-07:00) but moment gives -480 (-08:00)
  dayjs('2014-06-01T12:00:00Z').tz('America/Los_Angeles') -> dayjs -360 (-06:00) but moment -420 (-07:00)
  dayjs('2014-06-01T12:00:00Z').tz('Asia/Tokyo') -> dayjs +600 but moment +540
With the machine TZ=UTC everything matches. The error equals (machine's current UTC offset) - (machine's UTC offset at the target instant). The root cause is in `proto.tz`:

  const localUtcOffset = d().utcOffset()   // <-- captured ONCE at plugin load time (module scope)
  proto.tz = function (timezone) {
    const target = this.toDate().toLocaleString('en-US', { timeZone: timezone })
    const diff = (this.toDate() - new Date(target)) / 1000 / 60
    return d(target).utcOffset(localUtcOffset - diff, true)
  }

Read /dayjs/src/plugin/utc/index.js to understand `utcOffset(offset, keepLocalTime)` semantics before changing anything.

GOAL: `dayjs(x).tz(zone)` must match `moment(x).tz(zone)` (moment-timezone is installed) in `valueOf()`, `utcOffset()`, `format()` and `format('Z')`/`format('ZZ')`, for many zones and instants, INDEPENDENT of the machine's TZ. A robust approach is to compute the target offset directly from the instant with Intl (the plugin already has a `tzOffset(timestamp, timezone)` helper) and set `$offset` without changing the instant, e.g. build the result via `d(this.valueOf()).utcOffset(-tzOffset(this.valueOf(), timezone))` — but VERIFY empirically, do not guess. Keep the change minimal and keep `proto.tz` returning a dayjs instance whose valueOf equals the original instant.

